const GLOSSARY = [
  ["Absolute risk","The probability of an outcome in a defined group over a defined time. A relative effect needs a baseline risk before it can be translated into an absolute change."],
  ["Association","A statistical relationship between variables. It can be causal, but confounding, bias or reverse causation may also explain it."],
  ["Confidence interval","A range reflecting uncertainty around an estimate. A wide interval means the data are compatible with a broader set of effects."],
  ["Cross-sectional study","Exposure and outcome are assessed at one time point. It is useful for describing associations but weak for establishing what came first."],
  ["Crossover trial","Participants receive more than one intervention in sequence, acting partly as their own controls. Order and carryover can still matter."],
  ["Hazard ratio (HR)","A relative comparison of event rates over follow-up. HR 1.13 is a 13% relative difference—not a 13-point absolute increase."],
  ["Intention-to-treat","Analysis according to the assigned group, helping preserve the value of randomization even when adherence or completion differs."],
  ["NOVA / UPF","A processing-based classification. Ultra-processed foods are a heterogeneous category; the label does not capture every nutrient, context or purpose."],
  ["Preclinical","Research before adequate human clinical testing, often involving cells, tissues or animals. It can generate hypotheses but not establish a treatment recommendation."],
  ["Randomized controlled trial","Participants are assigned by chance to interventions, reducing some confounding. The conclusion still depends on sample, comparator, duration and outcome."],
  ["Residual confounding","Distortion that remains after statistical adjustment because some factors were unmeasured or imperfectly measured."],
  ["Scope of practice","The services a practitioner is legally, ethically and competently prepared to provide. Membership and tool access do not expand it."],
  ["Surrogate outcome","A marker used in place of a directly meaningful health outcome. A change in a marker does not automatically prove a change in symptoms, disease or survival."],
  ["Symptom questionnaire","A structured report that can organize discussion or monitor change. Unless validated and used by an authorized professional, it is not a diagnosis."],
  ["Statistical significance","A result unlikely under a specified null model, given assumptions. It does not by itself establish clinical importance, truth or equivalence."],
  ["Ultra-processed food","A NOVA category of industrial formulations. Research findings apply to the studied exposure and pattern, not automatically to every individual product."]
];

